------------------------------
Memory Injections
------------------------------

    Finding Malware
        - Malware runs in memory
        - We can find it using memory forensics
                - Memory contains running processes
    
    Memory Injection
        - Adding code into the memory of an existing process
        - Get access to the data in that process
            - Can lead to privilege escalation
    
        Most common form:
            DLL Injection -
                A windows library containing code and data, many apps use this library

                - Attackers inject a path to a malicious DLL
                    - When the app calls the path it gets injected by the DLL

                - One of the most popular, Pretty easy

------------------------------
Buffer Overflows
------------------------------
    The main jist is that it's overwritting a buffer of memory,and causes it to 
    spill over into other memory areas

    - Developers need to preform bounds checking, as the attackers spend alot of 
    time trying to find openings.

    - Not a simple exploit, takes time to avoid crashing the application

    - Really useful buffer overflow is repeatable, so once you're compromised 
    you're cooked.


------------------------------
Race Conditions
------------------------------
    When two events happen nearly at the same time, and the application
    has no idea that they might be happening simultaneously.
    
    - Devs commonly check, this can be bad if not planned for.
    - TOCTOU - Time-of-check to time-of-use attack
            App checks the system to retrieve information
            then after it may do a function with that value.

            But.. something may occur between when you grab that value and
            when you acutally use it.

            If you don't account for something between the check and the useage it 
            might be a place where we can abuse race condition.
    
--------------------------------
Software Updates
--------------------------------
    - Always keep your operating system and applications updated
        - Updates often include bug fixes and security patches
    
    - Not every update is equally secure
        - Attackers could have compromised the update

    - Follow best practices
        1. You should have a backup
        2. The source install should be from a trusted sources
        3. HAVE A BACKUP
    
    Downloading and updating
        - confirm the source, dont use weird websites
        - Visit the developer's site directly
        - Many Operating system will only allow signed apps, This ensures the app is trusted
    
    Automatic updates
        -Some applications will update Automatically
            - automatic verification
        - VERY HIGH TRUST - Comes directly from the developer

        - NOT 100% though.. the well can be poisoned by an attacker, but if thats the case
        its effecting ALL USERS.
    
